Article 13 A person shall meet the following conditions to become a public servant:
(1) Having the nationality of the People's Republic of China;
(2) Having reached the age of 18;
(3) Upholding the Constitution of the People's Republic of China, the leadership of the Communist Party of China, and the socialist system;
(4) Having good political quality and moral character;
(5) Having the physical conditions and psychological quality to perform duties normally;
(6) Having the educational level and work capability as required by the position;
(7) Other conditions as prescribed by law.

Article 14 A public servant shall perform the following obligations:
(1) Being loyal to the Constitution, observing and upholding the Constitution and laws in an exemplary manner, and consciously accepting the leadership of the Communist Party of China;
(2) Being loyal to the state, and safeguarding the state security, honor, and interests;
(3) Being loyal to the people, serving the people wholeheartedly, and accepting the supervision of the people;
(4) Being loyal to one's duties, being diligent and responsible, obeying and implementing the decisions and orders made by superiors according to law, performing duties within the prescribed authority and procedures, and striving to improve the quality and efficiency of work;
(5) Keeping state secrets and work secrets;
(6) Taking the lead in practicing the core socialist values, adhering to the rule of law, observing discipline, abiding by professional ethics, and setting an example in observing social morality and family virtues;
(7) Being honest, upright, and fair;
(8) Other obligations as prescribed by law.

Article 15 A public servant shall enjoy the following rights:
(1) Obtaining the working conditions necessary for performing duties;
(2) Not being removed from office, demoted, dismissed, or disciplined except for statutory reasons and through statutory procedures;
(3) Receiving remuneration, and enjoying benefits and insurance benefits;
(4) Participating in training;
(5) Making criticisms and suggestions regarding the work of the organ and its leading personnel;
(6) Lodging petitions and accusations;
(7) Applying for resignation;
(8) Other rights as prescribed by law.

Article 26 The following persons shall not be recruited as public servants:
(1) Those who have been subjected to criminal punishment for committing a crime;
(2) Those who have been expelled from the Communist Party of China;
(3) Those who have been expelled from public office;
(4) Those who have been included in the list of persons subject to joint disciplinary action for dishonesty according to law;
(5) Other circumstances specified by law that preclude recruitment as a public servant.

Article 46 The promotion of a civil servant to a leading position shall be handled in accordance with the following procedures:
(1) Motion;
(2) Democratic recommendation;
(3) Determination of candidates for investigation and organization of investigation;
(4) Discussion and decision in accordance with the administrative authority;
(5) Fulfillment of appointment formalities.

Article 52 Awards shall be given to a civil servant or a civil servant collective in any of the following circumstances:
(1) Being devoted to duty, working actively, taking responsibility, and achieving remarkable work results;
(2) Abiding by laws and disciplines, being honest and upright, having a correct work style, acting impartially, and playing an exemplary role;
(3) Making inventions or creations or putting forward rationalization proposals in work, achieving significant economic or social benefits;
(4) Making outstanding contributions to enhancing national unity and maintaining social stability;
(5) Achieving outstanding results in protecting public property and saving state assets;
(6) Performing meritorious services in preventing or eliminating accidents, thereby preventing or reducing losses to the interests of the state and the people;
(7) Making outstanding contributions in special circumstances such as emergency rescue and disaster relief;
(8) Performing meritorious services in combating violations of disciplines and laws;
(9) Winning honor and interests for the state in foreign exchanges;
(10) Having other outstanding meritorious achievements.

Article 56 An award shall be revoked in any of the following circumstances for a civil servant or a civil servant collective:
(1) Obtaining the award by fraud or deception;
(2) Concealing serious mistakes or seriously violating prescribed procedures when applying for the award;
(3) Having serious disciplinary or illegal acts that affect the reputation of the title;
(4) Other circumstances where the award shall be revoked as provided by laws or regulations.

Article 59 A civil servant shall abide by laws and disciplines, and shall not engage in any of the following acts:
(1) Spreading remarks that damage the authority of the Constitution, the prestige of the Communist Party of China, or the state, organizing or participating in activities such as assemblies, parades, or demonstrations aimed at opposing the Constitution, the leadership of the Communist Party of China, or the state;
(2) Organizing or participating in illegal organizations, or organizing or participating in strikes;
(3) Stirring up or undermining ethnic relations, participating in ethnic separatist activities, or organizing or exploiting religious activities to undermine ethnic unity and social stability;
(4) Being unaccountable, inactive, neglecting duties, or causing delays in work;
(5) Refusing to carry out decisions or orders made by superiors according to law;
(6) Suppressing or retaliating against criticism, appeals, accusations, or reports;
(7) Engaging in fraud, misleading or deceiving leaders and the public;
(8) Embezzling or taking bribes, using the convenience of one's position to seek personal gains for oneself or others;
(9) Violating financial discipline, wasting state assets;
(10) Abusing power, infringing upon the lawful rights and interests of citizens, legal persons, or other organizations;
(11) Disclosing state secrets or work secrets;
(12) Damaging national honor and interests in foreign exchanges;
(13) Participating in or supporting activities such as pornography, drug use, gambling, or superstition;
(14) Violating professional ethics, social morality, or family virtues;
(15) Violating relevant provisions by participating in prohibited online dissemination activities or online activities;
(16) Violating relevant provisions by engaging in or participating in for-profit activities, or holding concurrent positions in enterprises or other for-profit organizations;
(17) Being absent without leave, or failing to return without justifiable reasons after the expiration of business trips or leaves;
(18) Other acts that violate disciplines or laws.

Article 76 When a civil servant performs official duties, he or she shall withdraw in any of the following circumstances:
(1) where the matter involves his or her own interests;
(2) where the matter involves the interests of persons with whom he or she has a kinship relationship as listed in paragraph 1 of Article 74 of this Law;
(3) any other circumstance that may affect the impartial performance of official duties.

Article 86 A public servant shall not resign from public office under any of the following circumstances:
(1) The public servant has not completed the minimum service period prescribed by the state;
(2) The public servant holds a special position involving state secrets or has left such position but has not completed the declassification period prescribed by the state;
(3) Important official business has not yet been completed and must be continued by the public servant personally;
(4) The public servant is undergoing audit, disciplinary review, or supervisory investigation, or is suspected of committing a crime, and the judicial procedure has not yet concluded;
(5) Other circumstances in which resignation from public office is not permitted as provided for by laws and administrative regulations.

Article 88 A public servant shall be dismissed under any of the following circumstances:
(1) The public servant has been rated as incompetent in the annual assessment for two consecutive years;
(2) The public servant is not competent for the current work and refuses to accept other assignments;
(3) The public servant's organ is adjusted, abolished, merged, or its staffing is reduced, requiring a work adjustment, and the public servant refuses a reasonable arrangement;
(4) The public servant fails to perform the duties of a public servant, fails to comply with laws and public servant discipline, and after education still does not change, making the public servant unsuitable to continue working in the organ and not suitable for a sanction of discharge;
(5) The public servant is absent from work without leave, or after going on official business or taking leave, fails to return without justifiable reasons for more than fifteen consecutive days, or accumulates more than thirty days within one year.

Article 89 A public servant under any of the following circumstances shall not be dismissed:
(1) The public servant has been disabled in the line of duty and is confirmed to have lost or partially lost the ability to work;
(2) The public servant is ill or injured and is within the prescribed medical treatment period;
(3) The public servant is a female public servant during pregnancy, maternity leave, or breastfeeding period;
(4) Other circumstances in which dismissal is not permitted as provided for by laws and administrative regulations.

Article 93 Where a public servant meets any of the following conditions, and the public servant voluntarily submits an application and obtains approval from the appointing and removing organ, the public servant may retire early:
(1) The public servant has completed thirty years of service;
(2) The public servant is less than five years away from the retirement age prescribed by the state and has completed twenty years of service;
(3) Other circumstances in which early retirement is permitted as provided for by the state.

Article 95 Where a public servant refuses to accept the following personnel decisions concerning the public servant, the public servant may, within thirty days from the date of becoming aware of the personnel decision, apply for a review to the original decision-making organ; if the public servant refuses to accept the result of the review, the public servant may, within fifteen days from the date of receiving the review decision, file a petition to the public servant administration department at the same level or the organ at the next higher level over the organ that made the personnel decision, in accordance with regulations; or, without going through review, directly file a petition within thirty days from the date of becoming aware of the personnel decision:
(1) Sanctions;
(2) Dismissal or cancellation of recruitment;
(3) Demotion;
(4) Rating as incompetent in the regular assessment;
(5) Removal from office;
(6) Non-approval of application for resignation or early retirement;
(7) Failure to determine salary, welfare, or insurance benefits in accordance with regulations, or deduction thereof;
(8) Other circumstances in which a petition may be filed as provided for by laws and regulations.
Where a public servant refuses to accept the petition processing decision made by an organ below the provincial level, the public servant may file a further petition with the organ at the next higher level over the organ that made the decision.
The organ that accepts the petition of a public servant shall establish a fair petition committee for public servants, responsible for accepting and adjudicating petition cases of public servants.
Where a public servant refuses to accept a decision concerning the public servant made by a supervisory organ and applies to the supervisory organ for reconsideration or review, the matter shall be handled in accordance with the relevant provisions.

Article 106: Where any of the following circumstances violating the provisions of this Law occurs, the leading organ or the civil servant competent authority at or above the county level shall, within its administrative authority, order correction or declare such act void as the case may be; with respect to the responsible leading personnel and directly responsible personnel, depending on the seriousness of the circumstances, criticism and education, an order to make examination, admonishment, organizational adjustment, or sanction shall be imposed; where a crime is constituted, criminal liability shall be investigated according to law:
(1) failing to conduct recruitment, transfer, rotation, appointment by contract, or promotion of civil servants in accordance with the establishment quota, number of positions, or qualifications for holding a position;
(2) failing to conduct rewards and punishments, avoidance, or handling of retirement of civil servants in accordance with the prescribed conditions;
(3) failing to follow the prescribed procedures for recruitment, transfer, rotation, appointment by contract, promotion, assessment, rewards, and punishments of civil servants;
(4) violating state regulations by altering the standards for civil servants' remuneration, welfare, or insurance benefits;
(5) engaging in acts such as leaking examination questions, violating examination discipline, or other acts seriously affecting openness and fairness in the course of recruitment, open selection, etc.;
(6) failing to accept and handle civil servants' petitions and accusations in accordance with the provisions; or
(7) other circumstances violating the provisions of this Law.
